Function overloading allows multiple functions in the same scope to share the same name, provided they have different parameter lists (different number, type, or order of parameters). This enhances code readability and reusability.
```cpp
void print(int i) { /* ... */ }
void print(double d) { /* ... */ }
void print(const char* s) { /* ... */ }
```

Templates. C++ templates allow defining functions or classes that operate on generic types, specified as parameters. The compiler generates specific code for each type used at compile time. This avoids the need for `void*` and manual casting common in C for generic functions.
```cpp
template <typename T>
T maximum(T a, T b) {
    return (a > b) ? a : b;
}
```
